<h2>The marriage and the twin grounds</h2>
<p>The parties married at Dhanbad on 14 July 2021 under Hindu rites; there were no children. The husband&rsquo;s petition before the Family Court alleged that the wife suffered from a mental disorder dating back to her childhood which had been concealed from him, that she was abusive and violent — including knife attacks and attempts at self-harm — and that the marriage was never consummated. He sought dissolution on two statutory grounds: cruelty under Section 13(1)(i-a) and unsoundness of mind or mental disorder under Section 13(1)(iii) of the Hindu Marriage Act.</p>
<p>The wife denied every allegation, stated she was willing to continue the marriage, and countered that the husband&rsquo;s family had harassed her mentally. The Family Judge dismissed the petition in December 2024, holding that neither ground was proved. The husband appealed under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984; the wife did not appear to contest the appeal — which did not spare the appellant from proving his case.</p>

<h2>The cruelty ground: loud allegations, silent witnesses</h2>
<p>The appeal pressed the gravest allegations — knife attacks and suicide attempts — as proof of cruelty the trial court had supposedly ignored. The Division Bench&rsquo;s examination found the structure hollow:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>The hearsay problem</h3><p>The sole witness who spoke of the incidents had seen none of them; his account rested on what others had told him. The Bench held that such hearsay evidence cannot found a finding of matrimonial cruelty.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The admission problem</h3><p>The husband&rsquo;s own testimony showed the couple continued to cohabit in the United States until March 2023 — conduct difficult to reconcile with a household allegedly marked by knife violence.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The corroboration gap</h3><p>No contemporaneous complaint, medical record of injury, or independent account supported the pleaded incidents. Cruelty was asserted, not demonstrated.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The burden rule</h3><p>The ground belongs to the spouse who invokes it: the burden, the Bench reiterated, lies on the party who seeks to use the ground — and it never shifted here.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The mental disorder ground: no doctor, no diagnosis, no decree</h2>
<p>Section 13(1)(iii) is among the most demanding grounds in the statute. It is not enough that a spouse has some mental health condition; the disorder must be of such a kind and degree that the petitioner cannot reasonably be expected to live with the respondent — a threshold the Supreme Court has long policed strictly, cautioning that not every disorder justifies dissolution. Measured against that standard, the husband&rsquo;s case had nothing to stand on:</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>No medical certificate</strong> or treating doctor&rsquo;s records were produced at trial.</li>
  <li><strong>The hospital discharge papers did not mention schizophrenia</strong> — a point the husband himself conceded in evidence.</li>
  <li><strong>The wife&rsquo;s education and work history</strong> stood in the record, pointing away from an incapacitating disorder.</li>
  <li><strong>Concealment was alleged but never traced</strong> to any proved pre-marriage condition.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The Bench quoted the governing test — the disorder&rsquo;s &ldquo;degree must be such that the spouse seeking relief cannot reasonably be expected to live with the other&rdquo; — and found the record silent on both existence and degree.</p>

<h2>The allegations that went nowhere</h2>
<p>Two further strands of the husband&rsquo;s case deserve mention for what the judgment did <em>not</em> need to decide. The plea that the marriage was never consummated was pleaded alongside the other grounds but, like them, rested on assertion; non-consummation can in appropriate cases feed a cruelty analysis, but only when proved, and the record here proved nothing. The concealment narrative — that a childhood-onset disorder was suppressed before the marriage — similarly presupposed a diagnosed condition that the evidence never established. A fraud or concealment case under the Act&rsquo;s separate provisions carries its own limitation discipline and its own proof requirements; it cannot be smuggled into a cruelty petition as atmosphere.</p>

<h2>The appellate lens: perversity, not preference</h2>
<p>Because the appeal was a first appeal, the High Court was entitled to reappraise the entire record on facts and law. But reappraisal is not substitution. The Bench articulated the familiar discipline: a finding is perverse only if it ignores relevant material, rests on irrelevant material, or defies logic. The Family Judge had considered the evidence, applied the correct standards and reached a reasoned conclusion; the appellate court&rsquo;s task ended with that verification. The appeal was dismissed and the Family Court&rsquo;s judgment and decree upheld.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> A matrimonial petition should be drafted backwards from the witness box. Before pleading a knife attack, ask who saw it, what record exists, and whether the client&rsquo;s own timeline — residence, travel, continued cohabitation — is consistent with it. And before invoking Section 13(1)(iii), secure the medical foundation: a treating psychiatrist, diagnostic records, and proof of kind <em>and</em> degree. Grounds pleaded without their proof do not merely fail; they lend credibility to the opposing narrative.</p>
</div>
